SUMMARY:Baseball Night
DESCRIPTION:Tickets are officially on sale for the 2027 Mars Hill University Baseball Night fundraiser. Hosted by the 2026–2027 Lions baseball team\, this special evening brings our program’s biggest celebration of the year to downtown Asheville. \nGet tickets \nEvent Details: \nDate: Saturday\, January 16\, 2027\nTime: 6:00 PM – 9:00 PM\nLocation: HomeTrust Park (Downtown Asheville\, NC) \nFeaturing Keynote Speaker Jim Holland \nWe are thrilled to welcome Jim Holland—author\, civic leader\, and 2019 Appalachian League Hall of Fame inductee—as the keynote speaker for our 2027 event. \nJim brings insights from an extraordinary 30-season career (1990–2019) in southern West Virginia baseball. He is best known for his 24-year tenure (1992–2015) as General Manager of the Princeton\, WV Appalachian League franchise\, an affiliate of both the Cincinnati Reds (5 seasons) and Tampa Bay Rays (19 seasons). During his time in Princeton\, 85 future Major League players passed through his clubhouse. \nJim’s front-office tenure also included stops with the Huntington Cubs (1990–1991) and the summer collegiate West Virginia Miners of the Prospect League (2016–2019)\, as well as serving as Corporate Secretary and an executive committee member for the Appalachian League. \nCareer Accolades & Hall of Fame Honors: \n\nDouble Hall of Famer: Inducted into both the Appalachian League Hall of Fame—alongside legends like Cal Ripken Jr.\, Greg Maddux\, Eddie Murray\, Dale Murphy\, C.C. Sabathia\, Nolan Ryan\, and Andruw Jones—and the West Virginia Sports Legends/Artie Museum in 2019.\nLeague Recognition: Named Appalachian League Executive of the Year (1993)\, recipient of the Appalachian League President’s Award for long-term service (2015)\, and the only five-time winner of the league’s Award for Promotional Excellence.\nCommunity Leadership: Honored with the Rotary Club of Princeton’s Service Above Self Award and named the 2012 Citizen of the Year by the Princeton-Mercer County Chamber of Commerce.\n\nFollowing his retirement from baseball in May 2020\, Jim published his acclaimed memoir\, My Fortunate Detour (2022)\, chronicling his journey through the game. A 1976 two-sport letterman at Dunbar High School and a 1980 cum laude graduate of the University of Charleston (B.A. in Mass Communications)\, Jim resides in Princeton\, WV\, with his wife\, Judy. Their son\, Warren Hypes\, carries on the family’s baseball legacy as Vice President of Creative and Brand for the Tampa Bay Rays. \nJoin us at HomeTrust Park for an unforgettable night of baseball stories\, community\, and support for our student-athletes.
URL:https://www.mhu.edu/event/baseball-night-2/
LOCATION:McCormick Field\, 30 Buchanan Pl\, Asheville\, NC\, 28801\, United States
CATEGORIES:Alumni,Athletics,Baseball,Featured,Public Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.mhu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/baseball.jpg
END:VEVENT
